// THE PROGRAM OF BACKLOG ROW bir_flow_summary_seed_underreports (#447), and it
// is a FAIL fixture because the SHIPPED compile is correct: the counter-based
// checker refuses it. The ADR 0028 checker does not, and that is the defect —
// a hole that becomes a compile-time one the moment S6 switches the verdict.
//
// ONE VARIABLE against fail/bc_d1r2_ref_arg_hop_held, which both checkers
// refuse: a SECOND reference argument. With two, relate_call's `narrow` block
// consults the body summary, the summary answers `to_result=0` for a callee
// that plainly stores `b.p` into its result, every source is dropped, and the
// loan ties to nothing.
//
// ROOT, MEASURED: the summary's `taint_of` FieldRead arm short-circuits on
// `stored_shared_extract` (#77 r2 SEED). That is the right answer to the
// CHECKER's question — where does a new loan ROOT — and the wrong answer to the
// SUMMARY's — does a loan rooted at parameter i REACH the result. One predicate
// answering two notions. The repair is in borrow_flow_summary.inc, which the
// OLD checker also consumes, so it is priced on its own.
package bc_flow_hop_two_args_bir_admits;

#[borrow_carrying]
struct B<'a> { p: &'a i64 }
struct C { v: i64 }

impl C {
    fn mk(&self) -> B { return B { p: &self.v }; }
    fn bump(&mut self) { self.v = self.v + 1i64; }
}

fn thru2<'a>(b: &B<'a>, d: &B) -> B<'a> { return B { p: b.p }; }

fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut c: C = C { v: 1i64 };
    let b0: B = c.mk();
    let bx: B = c.mk();
    let b1: B = thru2(&b0, &bx);
    c.bump();
    return *b1.p;
}
